## 2.10 Collective expansion and the \(\mathbf{k_{t}}\)-dependence of measured HBT radii

If the particle momenta and emission points are correlated, the space-time variances in 2.24 depend on the pair momentum \(\mathbf{k}\). The most important mechanism to produce such correlations is collective expansion of the source. Random motion due to the finite temperature of the source tends to weaken the space-momentum correlations caused by collective flow. Assuming a rather general emission function the dependences of HBT radii on \(\mathbf{k}\) can be related to prominent features of the expansion and to the temperature of the source.

A widely used parametrization of the emission function is given by [10]

\[\mathrm{S\left(x,k\right)=\frac{m_{t}cosh\left(\eta-Y\right)}{8\,\pi^{4}\, \Delta\,\tau}\exp\left(-\frac{K\cdot u\left(x\right)}{T\left(x\right)}-\frac{ \left(\tau-\tau_{0}\right)^{2}}{2\left(\Delta\,\tau\right)^{2}}-\frac{r^{2}}{2 R^{2}}-\frac{\left(\eta-\eta_{0}\right)^{2}}{2\left(\Delta\,\eta\right)^{2}}\right)\]

\[\mathrm{Where}\quad r^{2}=x^{2}+y^{2}\,,\quad\eta=0.5\cdot\ln\left(\left(t+z \right)/\left(t-z\right)\right)\quad\mathrm{and}\quad\tau=\sqrt{t^{2}-z^{2}} \,\mathrm{ parametrize}\,\mathrm{ the}\,\mathrm{space}\,\mathrm{time}\,\mathrm{coordinates}\,\mathrm{\,x}^{\mu}\,,\,\mathrm{and}\quad Y=0.5\cdot\ln\left(\left(E+k\right)/ \left(E-k\right)\right)\,\mathrm{and}\quad m_{t}=\sqrt{m^{2}+k_{t}^{2}}\,\mathrm{ the}\,\mathrm{pair}\, \mathrm{momenta}\,\mathrm{k}.\]

\(R\) is the transverse radius of the source, \(\tau_{0}\,\) the average freeze out proper time and \(\Delta\,\tau\,\) the mean duration of particle emission. \(\Delta\,\eta\,\) accounts for the finite longitudinal extension of the source.

The space momentum correlations are introduced by the Boltzmann factor \(\exp\left(-k\cdot u\left(x\right)/T\left(x\right)\right)\) with a velocity field u(x) and a temperature T. Usually the velocity field is decomposed into

\[u\left(x\right)=\left(cosh\,\eta_{1}cosh\,\eta_{t},sinh\,\eta_{t}\,\mathbf{e} _{r}\,,\sinh\,\eta_{1}cosh\,\eta_{t}\right)\]

with longitudinal and transverse flow rapidities \(\eta_{1}\left(x\right)\,\) and \(\eta_{t}\left(x\right)\,\). In longitudinal direction, boost invariant flow is assumed \(\eta_{1}\left(x\right)=\eta\left(v_{1}=z\,/t\right)\,\) and in transverse direction the flow profile is parametrized by \(\eta_{t}\left(x\right)=\eta_{t}\cdot\left(r/R\right)\,\) with a scale parameter \(\eta_{t}\,\) and the transverse radius R.

Using the emission function given in equation 2.43 and employing the saddle point approximation 2.22, the characteristics of the source R, T, \(\tau_{0}\,,\,\) and \(\eta_{t}\,\)can be related to the \(\mathbf{k}-\)dependence of the measured HBT radii. According to [10] we find the following approximations:\[\mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{side}}\left(\mathrm{m}_{{}_{\mathrm{t}}}\right)\approx\frac{ \mathrm{R}}{\sqrt{1+\frac{\mathrm{\eta}_{\mathrm{f}}^{2}}{\mathrm{T}}\mathrm{m}_{{}_{ \mathrm{t}}}}} \tag{2.45}\]

\[\mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{long}}\left(\mathrm{m}_{{}_{\mathrm{t}}}\right)\approx \tau_{{}_{0}}\sqrt{\frac{\mathrm{T}}{\mathrm{m}_{{}_{\mathrm{t}}}}} \tag{2.46}\]

and

\[\mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{out}}^{{}^{2}}\left(\mathrm{k}\right)-\mathrm{R}_{ \mathrm{side}}^{{}^{2}}\left(\mathrm{k}\right)\approx\beta_{\mathrm{out}}^{{}^{2 }}\Delta\tau \tag{2.47}\]

These relations are highly model dependent, they rely on a number of approximations and assumptions, nevertheless they are often used to associate a specific physical meaning to extracted HBT radii. To test the validity of the approximations above, one has to check whether the resulting source parameters are consitent with other observations. E.g. temperature and transverse expansion also determine the shape of single particle transverse momentum spectra and can therefore be derived via this observable. Only if all observations fit into a consistent picture, we can draw conclusions about the early stages of a heavy ion collision and a possible phase transition.

### 2.11 Coulomb correction

The coulomb repulsion between like sign charged pions acting when they leave the collision region changes their relative momentum. This effect is corrected for by weighting the mixed pairs with a Coulomb correction factor. It is calculated by taking the square of the nonrelativistic wave function describing a particle in a Coulomb field [10]

\[\mathrm{H}\left(\mathrm{k},\mathrm{r}\right)=\frac{2\,\pi\,\eta}{\mathrm{e}^{ 2\,\pi\,\eta}-1}\left|\mathrm{F}\left(-\mathrm{i}\,\eta\,;1;\mathrm{ik}\left( \mathrm{r}-\frac{\mathrm{r}\cdot\mathrm{k}}{\mathrm{k}}\right)\right)\right|^{2} \tag{2.48}\]with

\[\text{F:}\text{confluent hypergeometric function}\] \[\mathbf{k}\,\text{:}\,\text{momentum of a pion in the pair c.m. system}\] \[\mathbf{r}\,\text{:}\,\text{relative distance of the two pions at freeze out}\] \[\eta\,\text{=}\frac{\text{Z}_{{}_{1}}\text{Z}_{{}_{2}}\text{m}_{{}_{ \pi}}\text{c}\,\alpha}{\text{q}_{{}_{\text{inv}}}}\] \[\text{m}_{{}_{\pi}}\text{:}\,\text{pion mass}\] \[\alpha\,\text{:}\,\text{fine structure constant}\]

For pions emitted from a pointlike source, F equals unity and the Coulomb correction becomes the Gamov factor [16]. Assuming a finite source size the correction can be calculated numerically. In Fig. 2.3 the correction is displayed for several source sizes.

